-- title:
--   A closed cube lies in the ball of radius $h\sqrt{d}$ about any of its points
-- statement:
--   Let $d \in \mathbb{N}$, let $h \in \mathbb{R}$, and let $u, x \in \mathbb{R}^d$. If $x$ belongs to the closed cube $\bar A_h(u)$ of side $h$ centred at $u$, then the whole cube is within distance $h\sqrt d$ of $x$:
--
--   $$x \in \bar A_h(u) \implies \bar A_h(u) \subseteq \bar B\bigl(x,\, h\sqrt{d}\bigr).$$
--
--   Unlike the bound about the *centre*, the base point here is an arbitrary point of the cube, and the radius is correspondingly doubled to the full diagonal $h\sqrt d$. This is exactly the comparison needed to make the family of cubes containing a given point a Vitali family: a cube of side $h$ containing $s$ sits inside $\bar B(s, h\sqrt d)$ while retaining the fixed volume $h^d$, a definite fraction of that ball's volume, which is what lets the Lebesgue differentiation theorem be applied along cubes that are not centred at the differentiation point.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** No positivity hypothesis on $h$ is required: if $h < 0$ the cube is empty and the hypothesis $x \in \bar A_h(u)$ cannot hold, making the statement vacuous. The centre $u$ is arbitrary — nothing requires it to be a lattice point.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/dbenbenn/quadratic-forms-sobolev/blob/7a1a680db2124d46ce370c91fd450aa454edf491/QuadraticFormsSobolev/LebesgueDiff.lean#L70-L86

